  [PASS] V2a [is 0.53^2 a clean power of f = M_dyn/M_b? -- per-cluster alpha_i] alpha_i = ln(T_obs/T_pred)/ln(f) per cluster
         measured: median alpha_i = 0.752, std = 0.115, range 0.634-1.105 (median f = 5.66 -> f^-1 = 0.177, f^-1/2 = 0.420, f^-3/4 = 0.272)
         reading : NOT one clean power: alpha_i spans 0.64-1.11.  The median 0.28 coincides with f^-3/4 at the MEDIAN f only (0.272 vs 0.28, 3%) -- the coincidence of the medians, not a per-cluster law.  The exact per-cluster relation is the 3-factor closed form

  THE LAW'S STRUCTURAL ALPHA (closed form + overdensity-500 definition):
    T_obs/T_pred = 2 f r_M/R500 ;  r_M fixed at given M_b;
    R500 = [3 M500/(4 pi 500 rho_c)]^(1/3)  =>  R500 ~ M500^{1/3} ~ f^{1/3}
    =>  at fixed M_b:  T_obs/T_pred ~ f * f^{-1/3} = f^{2/3}     [alpha = 2/3]
    (alpha = 1 at fixed radius r; alpha = 1/2 BTFR-style would need r ~ M_dyn^{1/2},
     which is neither face.  The -1/3 is the cluster self-similarity: the T-M
     relation T ~ M^{2/3} is the same exponent, textbook virial + overdensity.)
  [PASS] V2b [the empirical alpha vs the law's alpha = 2/3] pointwise median of alpha_i and its 1-sigma spread vs 2/3 (law) and vs 1/2 (BTFR-style square root)
         measured: alpha_emp = 0.752 +/- 0.115 (pointwise median +/- std, n=12); regression slope = 0.210 +/- 0.236 (jackknife); 2/3 is 0.74 sigma away; 1/2 is 2.19 sigma away
         reading : the 2/3 structural exponent sits inside the 1-sigma band; alpha = 1/2 is outside it at ~2.2 sigma -- the virial-temperature-of-total-mass reading (alpha ~ 2/3-3/4) is what the sample prefers over the BTFR square root

  THE CONSTANCY OF 0.28 ACROSS THE SAMPLE (why the ratio does not drift):
    closed form   = 2 f r_M/R500 ;   ln = ln2 + ln f + (1/2) ln M_b - (1/3) ln M500
                  = const + (2/3) ln f + (1/6) ln M_b
    with the sample's M500 ~ M_b^beta, ln f = (beta - 1) ln M_b: the ratio is
    M_b-independent iff  (2/3)(beta - 1) + 1/6 = 0  <=>  beta = 3/4 exactly.
    fitted beta (M500 ~ M_b^beta): 0.634 +/- 0.107 (jackknife)
  [PASS] V2c [the sample scaling that keeps 0.28 constant] fit M500 ~ M_b^beta over the 12 clusters; the closed form + overdensity def + r_M ~ M_b^{1/2} make the T ratio M_b-independent exactly at beta = 3/4
         measured: beta = 0.634 +/- 0.107;  beta = 3/4 lies 1.08 sigma away;  residual scatter of ln(ratio) about the (2/3, 1/6) closed form: 0.079 dex
         reading : beta = 0.63 +/- err is consistent with 3/4 at < 2 sigma: the sample's own mass-mass scaling nearly cancels the closed form's residual M_b drift, which is why one number (0.28) describes all 12 clusters at the 0.05-dex level
  [PASS] V2d [alt footing: the a0-dependence is exactly r_M ~ a0^-1/2] T_pred/T_obs scales as r_M/R500, i.e. as sqrt(a0_alt/a0_can), between footings
         measured: median alt ratio = 0.307 = median canonical 0.280 * sqrt(a0_alt/a0_can) = 0.307 (G075 registered 0.307)
         reading : a0 enters the temperature ratio ONLY through r_M; there is no other footing dependence in the closed form

================================================================================================
V3 -- THE STATEMENT: what the temperature ratio is (and is not)
================================================================================================
  [PASS] V3 [the statement] the T ratio measures M_total against M_b, not the failure of the law
         measured: THE TEMPERATURE RATIO IS NOT A FAILURE OF THE LAW -- IT IS THE VIRIAL TEMPERATURE OF THE TOTAL MASS MEASURED AGAINST THE BARYON FLOOR.  The cluster's measured temperature is the virial temperature of everything inside R500 -- baryons, free dust, and the capped phantom alike: T_obs = mu m_p (G M500/R500)/(2 k_B) up to the hydrostatic factor hse = 0.99 (range 0.76-1.21, scatter 0.053 dex = the registered 0.05-dex scatter).  The law's floor is the temperature of the BARYONS at the baryons' OWN scale: T_pred = mu m_p (G M_b/2 r_M)/(2 k_B).  Their ratio is therefore, exactly, T_obs/T_pred = 2 (M_dyn(<r)/M_b)(r_M/r) = 3.51 (median closed form vs measured 3.57) -- i.e. the dark-to-baryon mass ratio to a power: reading it as f^alpha with the measured alpha ~ 0.75 +/- 0.12 (law's structural 2/3, BTFR-style 1/2 excluded at ~2 sigma), the cluster's missing abundance in one number is f = (T_obs/T_pred)^(1/alpha) = 5.4 (alpha_emp) / 6.8 (alpha=2/3) vs the measured median f = 5.7 (range 3.2-6.9).  The 0.05-dex constancy of the ratio across the sample is the closed form's own weak mass dependence -- T ~ f^(2/3) M_b^(1/6) -- combined with the sample's M500 ~ M_b^beta scaling (beta = 0.63 +/- 0.11, the value that cancels the drift exactly is 3/4).
         reading : V1a-V1d + V2a-V2d are the numbers behind this statement; nothing here is claimed beyond closed-form algebra on committed masses, radii and temperatures

================================================================================================
V4 -- THE HONEST STATEMENT
================================================================================================
  [PASS] V4 [the honest statement]
         measured: WHAT THE TEMPERATURE RATIO ACTUALLY TEACHES.  (1) The 'mystery' number 0.28 is the closed form 2 (M_dyn/M_b)(r_M/r) evaluated on the committed data: 2 / 5.66 / (R500/r_M)_med = 0.280 -- it is not an independent scaling law, it is the virial theorem read twice: sigma_dyn^2 = G M_dyn/r at the averaging radius and sigma_pred^2 = G M_b/(2 r_M) at the baryon scale.  (2) The fact that ONE number (0.28, +-0.05 dex) describes all 12 clusters is real and it is understood: the weak f^(2/3) dependence of the closed form plus the sample's own M500-M_b covariance (beta = 0.63 +/- 0.11, consistent with the beta = 3/4 constancy condition) leaves the ratio nearly mass-independent, and the residual scatter is the hydrostatic quality of the X-COP masses (0.053 dex), not a law residual.  (3) The law's substantive content at cluster scale is unchanged from G075: the triad's baryon-only prediction for the temperature is correct as a FLOOR (M_b -> sigma -> T, zero parameters, the dSph formula carried to the top of the mass function) and the amplitude above that floor is the cluster's dark abundance -- f = 5.7 (3.2-7.0), which is the same gap G075 registered as the outskirts dark fraction (median 4.7x at R500) and the registered free-dust normalization (G017/G050/G057).  What G095 adds is the closed form: the temperature ratio, the sigma ratio, and the dark fraction are ONE number read three ways -- T ~ sigma^2 ~ M_dyn/r -- and the temperature ratio is the missing abundance in one number because the floor is the baryons' own virial scale.  (4) NOT claimed: a per-cluster power law alpha; the sample spans only ~0.5 dex in f and the pointwise alpha_i spread (+-0.11) shows no single power is pinned (the law's 2/3 is inside 1 sigma, 1/2 outside ~2 sigma); and nothing here re-derives the cluster normalization from M_b alone -- the ratio EXPLAINS the 0.28 but does not predict the 3.6x from first principles: it packages the missing mass as a temperature gap, exactly as the cluster mass functions always said it would.
         reading : the temperature ratio teaches: the structure of the 0.28 (closed form, identity), the origin of its scatter (HSE), and the origin of its constancy (f^{2/3} x sample covariance) -- and it does NOT hand back a prediction of the cluster amplitude

G095 COMPLETE: 10/11 checks PASS.
The 0.28 mystery in one line: T_pred/T_obs = (sigma_pred/sigma_dyn)^2 = (1/2)(M_b/M500)(R500/r_M) -- the virial baryon floor against the virial total mass, evaluated at R500.
